# Bandura Fracture Club

**Ingredients:** UKRAINIAN BANDURA + DECONSTRUCTED CLUB / POST-CLUB + PREPARED BASS DRUM + GLASS MARIMBA + BASS RECORDER + SHORT ANALOG SUB

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a late-night acoustics residency inside a decommissioned structural-load laboratory in Kyiv, where bandura player Oksana Melnyk was recording isolated plucked-string clusters beneath suspended test frames while producer Mira Voss built sparse post-club percussion from impact samples, relay clicks, and truncated bass tones. The useful connection appeared when Mira stopped using the bandura as a continuous harmonic bed and instead arranged the track around the instrument's rapid decay hierarchy: some treble strings vanished almost instantly, some bass strings carried longer resonance, and selected multi-string sweeps left a cloud of uneven after-ringing. Prepared bass drum, glass marimba, and bass recorder were added to create a club language where rhythm is determined by which resonance dies first.

Bandura Fracture Club is an acoustic-electronic form built around uneven decay. Deconstructed club supplies the structural freedom: asymmetrical kick placement, abrupt silences, sub pulses, clipped metallic percussion, destabilized bar lengths, isolated transients, and sections that imply dance-floor momentum without preserving a conventional loop. The bandura supplies a dense but sharply articulated plucked field whose individual strings decay at different rates.

The bandura is recorded with one stereo pair above the treble string field, a close microphone near the bass strings, and a contact microphone on the soundboard. The stereo pair captures directionality across the string fan. The bass microphone preserves low pluck body and sympathetic resonance. The contact microphone captures nail attack, wood vibration, damping pressure, and residual string buzz. The three sources remain distinct enough that different registers can carry separate timing functions.

Players favor short arpeggiated fans, open fifths, minor seconds, modal thirds, octave spreads, repeated tonic clusters, muted bass-string attacks, high treble pinpricks, alternating left- and right-hand damping, and sudden full-hand sweeps that are immediately stopped. Long virtuosic runs are minimized. The important gesture is not speed but the pattern by which resonance disappears.

The central compositional device is the **Decay Ladder**. Each important bandura figure is divided into three audible stages: STRIKE, RESIDUE, and VOID. STRIKE is the initial plucked attack. RESIDUE is the surviving mixture of ringing strings after the brightest transients vanish. VOID is the point where only room tone or extremely faint sympathetic vibration remains. Electronic events are written around these stages. A hard club impact may answer STRIKE, a glass-marimba dyad may appear during RESIDUE, and a sub pulse may be delayed until the sound reaches VOID. The result is a groove organized by acoustic disappearance rather than note onset alone.

Deconstructed-club percussion is dry at the source. Kicks are compact, occasional, and often separated by long irregular gaps. Claps and snares are brittle and may appear slightly ahead of or behind the apparent beat. Small metal hits, cable snaps, relay clicks, rubber impacts, and filtered noise bursts are used as transient material, but no layer is allowed to become continuous texture. The arrangement repeatedly exposes empty space.

Prepared bass drum supplies the acoustic low-frequency shock. Felt, cloth, removable damping, small weights resting safely on the shell hardware, and hand pressure on the head create short thuds, dry skin knocks, muted boom, and scraped resonance. The acoustic drum does not reinforce every electronic kick. Selected programmed low hits are removed completely so one bass-drum event owns that structural position.

Glass marimba provides a brittle fixed-pitch counterfield. Its bars are tuned glass or glass-ceramic elements mounted with short natural decay. The player uses soft rubber and yarn mallets, finger damping, and isolated two-note shapes. Open fifths, minor sevenths, suspended seconds, and narrow minor-second pairs are favored. The instrument often enters when the bandura has lost its high frequencies but still retains low-string residue.

Bass recorder occupies the low-mid air register. It contributes breath-heavy fundamentals, key noise, overblown partials, short descending fourths, and unpitched air. The recorder enters mainly after bandura sweeps, when the plucked field has already thinned. Long lyrical woodwind melody is avoided.

Analog sub remains mono, short, and delayed. It should rarely begin at the same moment as a bandura attack. Instead, the low-frequency response often arrives after the acoustic event has substantially decayed, creating the sensation that bass pressure is occupying the space left behind rather than reinforcing the original note.

Meter is flexible but not arbitrary. Most sections remain in 4/4 or compound 8-beat frames, yet individual events can be displaced by eighth-note or sixteenth-note amounts, and occasional bars may be shortened or extended by one subdivision. These disruptions are tied to audible decay events rather than inserted as abstract complexity.

The defining tension is density versus vacancy. The bandura can produce many simultaneous strings in one gesture, but the club arrangement becomes most powerful when it waits for those strings to disappear one by one.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 122-136 BPM, usually 4/4 with asymmetrical kick placement, clipped snares, isolated club impacts, occasional shortened or extended bars, prepared-bass-drum substitutions, and long exposed spaces between major events.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian, Aeolian, Ukrainian modal inflections, open fifths, minor 7ths, suspended 2nds, octave spreads, minor-second clusters, pedal tones, and slow bass-note movement. Harmony is typically static while register and decay create motion.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Decay Ladder** — bandura gestures move through STRIKE, RESIDUE, and VOID; different electronic or acoustic layers are assigned to each stage.
2. **Late Sub Entry** — mono sub frequently enters only after the plucked field has nearly disappeared.
3. **Glass Residue Marker** — glass-marimba notes appear during the middle decay stage, when bright bandura transients are gone but low resonance remains.
4. **Bass-Drum Exchange** — selected electronic kicks are removed completely and replaced by one dry prepared-bass-drum event.
5. **Register Separation** — treble and bass bandura strings are captured and mixed as distinct rhythmic zones rather than one stereo wash.
6. **No-Sustain Shortcut** — long pads, shimmer reverbs, and stretched bandura samples may not be used to fake continuity; continuity must come from real string resonance or clearly written instrumental sustain.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through larger bandura string fans, closer fracture events, harder prepared-bass-drum attacks, brighter glass-marimba strikes, stronger recorder overblows, and shorter VOID gaps. Peaks remain discontinuous and percussive rather than becoming walls of sound.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Bandura Fracture Club formed inside a retired structural-load and materials laboratory on the industrial edge of Kyiv. The building retained steel test frames, hydraulic jacks, dial gauges, cracked safety glass, numbered specimen racks, plywood worktops, cable trays, overhead rails, white inspection cards, and amber emergency lamps.

Bandura player **Oksana Melnyk** had been invited to document how different damping techniques changed the decay of individual string groups. She was particularly interested in wide sweeps where high strings vanished first while lower strings continued to resonate.

Producer **Mira Voss** was using an adjoining control room to develop sparse deconstructed-club material. Her first attempt layered Oksana's bandura over a finished beat and sounded ornamental. Oksana instead played one broad sweep and muted only the treble strings. Mira removed the next kick and waited until the remaining bass resonance disappeared before introducing a sub pulse. The empty interval felt more rhythmic than the original loop.

Percussionist **Taras Holub** joined with a large bass drum prepared for extremely short low-frequency attacks. Glass-marimba player **Iryna Datsenko** added fixed brittle tones that could occupy the middle decay zone without competing with plucked attacks. Bass-recorder player **Jon Bell** completed the group by supplying low air and breath tone after the acoustic strings had thinned.

Their first performances were called **load-release tests**. A simple vertical diagram behind the group showed three horizontal zones labeled STRIKE, RESIDUE, VOID. No sensors were used. The musicians listened to the natural decay and entered according to the stage they heard.

The visual language developed around safety-glass green, brushed steel, plywood beige, graphite black, inspection-card white, hydraulic red, dark wool, and dim amber emergency lighting. The unofficial rule is that no new layer should enter merely because the bar changed; it must enter because something else stopped resonating.

