## 3. Void Starts When the Last Bass String Stops

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 124 BPM with almost no drums. Oksana plays low bandura strings one at a time and lets each decay completely. Mira places tiny relay clicks only during STRIKE and keeps sub absent for the first two minutes. Iryna uses heavily damped glass notes that disappear almost immediately, making the bandura feel unusually long-lived by comparison.

Taras enters with one soft prepared-bass-drum pulse after every second full decay. Jon plays low recorder breath during RESIDUE but stops before VOID. As the track progresses, Oksana increases the number of simultaneous strings while shortening each phrase through damping, so acoustic density rises even as average decay becomes shorter.

In the final section, every instrument prepares an expected response to one last low bandura note. The note rings longer than rehearsal. Nobody enters. When the last audible bass string finally stops, Mira allows a single analog sub pulse to occupy the complete VOID for less than half a beat. The recording cuts immediately after it.

